WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:03.167
-These days, the news stinks.
I don't have to tell you that.

00:00:03.167 --> 00:00:05.467
Every headline I see makes me
feel worse than the last.

00:00:05.467 --> 00:00:06.868
Every once in a while,

00:00:06.868 --> 00:00:08.701
there's a story
that makes me feel great.

00:00:08.701 --> 00:00:11.100
And I'd like to share one
in a segment called

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.200
"The Kind of Story
We Need Right Now."

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.868
[ Theme music plays ]

00:00:15.868 --> 00:00:17.234
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.234 --> 00:00:19.767
This is a Eurasian eagle-owl,

00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:21.934
but it's not just any
Eurasian eagle-owl.

00:00:21.934 --> 00:00:24.400
This owl's name is Flaco,
and last month,

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.434
he was minding his own business,
living in the Central Park Zoo,

00:00:27.434 --> 00:00:30.334
when some vandals
cut a hole in his enclosure.

00:00:30.334 --> 00:00:33.834
And Flaco did what any
self-respecting owl in captivity

00:00:33.834 --> 00:00:35.734
would do -- he escaped.
That's right.

00:00:35.734 --> 00:00:38.434
Flaco Shawshanked
out of that cage, which...

00:00:38.434 --> 00:00:40.234
[ Laughter ]
...according to one article,

00:00:40.234 --> 00:00:42.200
was the size of a bus stop

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.234
and which he'd been living in
for 12 years,

00:00:44.234 --> 00:00:46.167
and he started flying
around Central Park.

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:48.400
This is the kind of story
we need right now.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:49.834
Every New Yorker dreams

00:00:49.834 --> 00:00:52.300
of moving out of their tiny
[Bleep] apartment,

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.000
into a nicer neighborhood,
and Flaco did it.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.067
Every New Yorker wants to live
on Central Park?

00:00:59.067 --> 00:01:01.767
Suddenly, this dude was living
in Central Park,

00:01:01.767 --> 00:01:04.434
without a trust fund.
Incredible.

00:01:04.434 --> 00:01:07.534
Also Flaco had been living
next to the penguin enclosure,

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:09.100
and if there's one thing
every New Yorker

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.667
has had to put up with
at some point,

00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:13.434
it's living next to somebody
who eats a ton of fish.

00:01:13.434 --> 00:01:17.033
My man Flaco
escaped a nightmare.

00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:18.100
[ Laughter ]

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.734
Not long after Flaco
left the zoo,

00:01:19.734 --> 00:01:21.133
he was spotted on 5th Avenue,

00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:23.634
peering in the window
at Bergdorf Goodman.

00:01:23.634 --> 00:01:25.300
Of course he was.
He spent 12 years

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.701
living in Manhattan,
never got to see the sights.

00:01:27.701 --> 00:01:29.868
[ Laughter ]
That's why he was also spotted

00:01:29.868 --> 00:01:32.734
at the Statue of Liberty,
at the M&amp;M's store,

00:01:32.734 --> 00:01:34.834
and in the front row
at "Hamilton."

00:01:34.834 --> 00:01:36.434
There he is.
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:01:36.434 --> 00:01:38.400
Probably a montage of him
somewhere,

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.868
trying on a bunch of outfits.

00:01:40.868 --> 00:01:43.534
Flaco's escape created
an exciting opportunity

00:01:43.534 --> 00:01:45.400
for him to spread his wings.
But more importantly,

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:49.267
it created an opportunity
for reporters to say owl puns.

00:01:49.267 --> 00:01:51.367
-It's a hoo-dunnit.
-Hoo-dunnit.

00:01:51.367 --> 00:01:53.901
-Hoo-hoo-hoo-dunnit.
[ Light laughter ]

00:01:53.901 --> 00:01:56.434
-It's a "How did you not come up
with something

00:01:56.434 --> 00:01:58.701
better than that?"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:58.701 --> 00:02:00.367
I love that they don't even
come up with different puns.

00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:01.801
It's like they're all using
the same copy

00:02:01.801 --> 00:02:03.467
of "Bartlett's Puns
for Reporters."

00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:05.801
But the best part
of this whole story is,

00:02:05.801 --> 00:02:07.968
everybody underestimated Flaco.
When he first escaped,

00:02:07.968 --> 00:02:10.334
an owl expert
told "The New York Times"

00:02:10.334 --> 00:02:11.801
that Flaco
would be easy to catch,

00:02:11.801 --> 00:02:15.167
because in his experience, owls
were not particularly smart.

00:02:15.167 --> 00:02:17.200
Oh, yeah?
Then why are they

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.734
the only birds who graduate?
[ Laughter ]

00:02:20.734 --> 00:02:23.167
Authorities were worried
Flaco wouldn't be able

00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:24.400
to feed himself in the wild,

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.367
because he'd spent
his whole life in captivity.

00:02:26.367 --> 00:02:29.367
Well, guess what owls eat?
Rodents and birds.

00:02:29.367 --> 00:02:32.133
Worrying an owl will starve
in Central Park

00:02:32.133 --> 00:02:33.934
is like worrying a toddler
will starve

00:02:33.934 --> 00:02:36.734
in a chicken-nugget factory.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:36.734 --> 00:02:39.934
Flaco did just fine on his own
because Flaco's not a dumbass,

00:02:39.934 --> 00:02:42.801
but zoo officials were still
determined to get him back.

00:02:42.801 --> 00:02:44.567
So they set up
an elaborate ruse.

00:02:44.567 --> 00:02:47.901
They left a dead rat
out in an open field,

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:50.133
and they put it inside a trap.

00:02:50.133 --> 00:02:53.767
And then they played a recording
of owl mating calls

00:02:53.767 --> 00:02:55.634
to lure Flaco to it.

00:02:55.634 --> 00:02:57.567
And you know what happened next?

00:02:57.567 --> 00:02:59.634
Flaco swooped down,
grabbed the rat,

00:02:59.634 --> 00:03:01.968
but didn't stay long enough
to get caught in the trap.

00:03:01.968 --> 00:03:05.934
But he did stay long enough
to look directly at a camera...

00:03:05.934 --> 00:03:08.701
[ Laughter ]
...and pose.

00:03:08.701 --> 00:03:11.133
Look at his face.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:11.133 --> 00:03:13.067
I don't know Flaco,
but I'm pretty sure

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:15.601
that face is saying, "Do I look
like a [ bleep ] idiot?"

00:03:15.601 --> 00:03:17.701
[ Laughter ]

00:03:17.701 --> 00:03:19.701
The zoo officials are like,

00:03:19.701 --> 00:03:21.467
"We shouldn't tell anybody
about this."

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:23.267
[ Light laughter ]

00:03:23.267 --> 00:03:25.200
They made several more attempts
to catch Flaco.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.067
They followed him around
Central Park with nets.

00:03:27.067 --> 00:03:29.367
The NYPD even tried to catch him
with a dog crate.

00:03:29.367 --> 00:03:31.033
Nothing worked.
And pretty soon,

00:03:31.033 --> 00:03:32.701
New Yorkers started putting
pressure on officials

00:03:32.701 --> 00:03:34.400
to just leave Flaco alone.
And after weeks

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.434
of trying to catch him,
zoo officials did just that.

00:03:36.434 --> 00:03:38.901
They announced they would end
their attempts to capture Flaco,

00:03:38.901 --> 00:03:41.033
and let him be free.
This is the kind of story

00:03:41.033 --> 00:03:43.968
we need right now.
Everyone underestimated Flaco.

00:03:43.968 --> 00:03:45.834
[ Cheers and applause ]
They said he couldn't survive

00:03:45.834 --> 00:03:47.334
on his own.
He did it.

00:03:47.334 --> 00:03:48.934
This is a story
for the underdogs,

00:03:48.934 --> 00:03:50.234
a story for anybody
who's ever been told,

00:03:50.234 --> 00:03:51.634
"You're too short
to play basketball,"

00:03:51.634 --> 00:03:52.968
or "Girls can't do math,"

00:03:52.968 --> 00:03:55.367
or "J.Lo is never gonna date you
a second time."

00:03:55.367 --> 00:03:58.167
So, tonight, if you walk
through Central Park,

00:03:58.167 --> 00:03:59.601
look up at the sky.
You just might see

00:03:59.601 --> 00:04:00.801
something amazing.
You might see

00:04:00.801 --> 00:04:03.334
Flaco flying free,
with a rat from one talon

00:04:03.334 --> 00:04:05.834
and a "Hamilton" playbill
in the other,

00:04:05.834 --> 00:04:09.100
'cause this is the kind of story
we need right now.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.467
[ Theme music plays ]

